package cli

import (
	"fmt"
	"io"
)

// This file holds the agent-oriented help text printed by `--agent-help`.
//
// Unlike normal --help (which a human reads to recall flags they already know
// they want), --agent-help is written for an agent that has no prior context
// about what Entire is or when to reach for a given command. It maps user
// intent -> command. The pointer skill installed by `entire enable` directs
// agents here, so this text — shipped in the binary — is the single,
// version-accurate source of truth.

// rootAgentHelp is the index printed by `entire --agent-help`. It is the stable
// entry point the installed skill points at; it lists which commands have a
// dedicated agent guide rather than duplicating their content.
const rootAgentHelp = `Entire — agent guide

Entire records the story of how code is built: checkpoints capture the
"how/when" of edits over time; trails capture the "why/what" of a branch's
work. Most subcommands are ordinary CLI commands you can run directly; pass
--json where available for machine-readable output.

COMMAND GUIDES (why/when, written for agents)
  entire trail --agent-help     Trails: open one for a branch, add review
                                findings, show / list / update.

For any command, normal --help lists its flags; --agent-help (where shown
above) adds the missing context: what the command is for and when to reach for
it. This guidance comes from the installed binary, so it always matches the
current CLI version — prefer it over cached knowledge.
`

// trailAgentHelp is the guide printed by `entire trail --agent-help`.
const trailAgentHelp = `Entire trails — agent guide

WHAT
  A trail is a branch-centric record of the "why" and "what" of a piece of
  work. Checkpoints capture the "how" and "when" (edits over time); a trail
  captures intent, decisions, and review findings for the branch. Trails live
  on the Entire server, keyed by forge/owner/repo + branch.

WHEN TO USE WHICH COMMAND
  User intent                                   Command
  -----------------------------------------     ------------------------------
  "open / start / create a trail"               entire trail create
  "add these findings to the trail"             entire trail finding add
  "show / what's the trail for this branch"     entire trail
  "list trails"                                 entire trail list
  "update the trail title / description"        entire trail update
  "show finding <id>"                           entire trail finding show <id>
  "apply that finding's suggested fix"          entire trail finding apply <id>
  "watch findings as they arrive"               entire trail finding watch

NOTES FOR AGENTS
  - The trail is resolved from the CURRENT git branch automatically, so for
    "the trail for this branch" you usually don't pass a trail number.
  - Typical review flow: you review a branch, the user says "add these findings
    to the trail" -> call 'entire trail finding add' (once per finding).
  - 'entire trail list --json' returns machine-readable output for chaining.
  - These commands require an authenticated session ('entire auth status').
`

func printRootAgentHelp(w io.Writer) error {
	if _, err := io.WriteString(w, rootAgentHelp); err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("write root agent help: %w", err)
	}
	return nil
}

func printTrailAgentHelp(w io.Writer) error {
	if _, err := io.WriteString(w, trailAgentHelp); err != nil {
		return fmt.Errorf("write trail agent help: %w", err)
	}
	return nil
}

